  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 5,
    "lesson_title": "Converting a Phasor Sum Back to a Sinusoid",
    "lesson_core_move": "Convert a polar phasor sum back into a single same-frequency sinusoid by mapping magnitude to amplitude and angle to phase at the common angular frequency.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 5,
        "lesson_title": "Converting a Phasor Sum Back to a Sinusoid",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Converting a Phasor Sum Back to a Sinusoid",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "When a polar phasor sum is written as $A\\angle\\phi$ at a common angular frequency $\\omega_0$, the conversion target is one same-frequency cosine. Read the magnitude as amplitude, read the angle as phase, and keep the angular frequency unchanged.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "1\\angle 0 \\leftrightarrow \\cos(\\omega_0 t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The base case shows the defaults: magnitude $1$ becomes amplitude $1$, and angle $0$ becomes zero phase. Nothing changes in the frequency term, so the same $\\omega_0$ stays outside the phasor-to-cosine mapping.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "A\\angle\\phi \\leftrightarrow A\\cos(\\omega_0 t + \\phi)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the rule is simple: magnitude becomes amplitude, angle becomes phase, and the shared $\\omega_0$ stays fixed. The next examples in this topic will keep that same mapping while isolating amplitude, phase, frequency, and then the full conversion.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 5,
        "lesson_title": "Converting a Phasor Sum Back to a Sinusoid",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Identifying the Sinusoid Amplitude from Phasor Magnitude",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "A phasor sum is $7\\angle 25^\\circ$ at common angular frequency $\\omega_0$. Identify the amplitude of the equivalent same-frequency cosine.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue in this step is the phasor magnitude. Here the phasor sum is $7\\angle 25^\\circ$ at common angular frequency $\\omega_0$, so the number to read first is the $7$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "A = 7",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Match that magnitude to the amplitude of the equivalent same-frequency cosine. The angle $25^\\circ$ stays in the background for this step, and the common angular frequency $\\omega_0$ is unchanged.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the amplitude is $7$. This example isolates only the magnitude-to-amplitude mapping; the phase and full cosine form are handled in later steps.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 5,
        "lesson_title": "Converting a Phasor Sum Back to a Sinusoid",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Identifying the Sinusoid Phase from Phasor Angle",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "A phasor sum is $4\\angle -\\pi/3$ at common angular frequency $\\omega_0$. Identify the phase of the equivalent same-frequency cosine.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue in this step is the phasor angle. Here the phasor sum is $4\\angle -\\pi/3$ at common angular frequency $\\omega_0$, so the angle to read is $-\\pi/3$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\phi = -\\pi/3",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Match that angle to the phase of the equivalent same-frequency cosine. Preserve the sign exactly, and keep the amplitude $4$ and the common angular frequency $\\omega_0$ unchanged for this step.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the phase is $-\\pi/3$. This example isolates only the angle-to-phase mapping; the full cosine form is handled later.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 5,
        "lesson_title": "Converting a Phasor Sum Back to a Sinusoid",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Preserving the Common Angular Frequency in the Sinusoid",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "A phasor sum is $3\\angle \\pi/6$ at common angular frequency $\\omega_0=4$. Write the matching cosine and keep the frequency exactly as given.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue in this step is the common angular frequency. Here the phasor sum is $3\\angle \\pi/6$ at common angular frequency $\\omega_0=4$, so the frequency term stays fixed at $4$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t) = 3\\cos(4t+\\pi/6)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Keep that $4$ unchanged in the cosine argument. The magnitude $3$ gives the amplitude and the angle $\\pi/6$ gives the phase; this step only checks that the frequency is preserved exactly as stated.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the matching cosine is $x(t)=3\\cos(4t+\\pi/6)$. No new frequency appears, and no existing frequency term is changed.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 5,
        "lesson_title": "Converting a Phasor Sum Back to a Sinusoid",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Writing the Complete Cosine from a Polar Phasor Sum",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "A phasor sum is $9\\angle -\\pi/4$ at common angular frequency $\\omega_0=2$. Write the complete equivalent cosine.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue in this step is the complete conversion. Read the magnitude as the amplitude, read the angle as the phase, and carry the common angular frequency through unchanged.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t) = 9\\cos(2t-\\pi/4)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Here the phasor sum is $9\\angle -\\pi/4$ at common angular frequency $\\omega_0=2$, so the amplitude is $9$, the phase is $-\\pi/4$, and the frequency term stays $2t$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the complete equivalent cosine is $x(t)=9\\cos(2t-\\pi/4)$. This final example combines the three isolated mappings from the earlier steps into one answer.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
